This gives ordering + once-guard. seq is assigned inside the closure... hmm, seq should be assigned at enqueue time or send time? `_nextSeq()` must be called in report order to preserve strictly increasing seq. Since the chain executes in enqueue order, calling `_nextSeq()` inside the closure still preserves order. But `state()` calls `_nextSeq()` at build time (enqueue). Mixed approaches are fine as long as actual call order of `_nextSeq` matches enqueue order. With release, `_nextSeq()` inside the closure runs when the chain reaches it — which is still after all previously-enqueued state reports' `_nextSeq()` calls (those run at their enqueue, which happened-before). And a state report enqueued AFTER release was requested... can't happen — after `_released = true`, no more state reports? Actually state() doesn't check `_released`. Hmm — should state() after release be suppressed? The once-guard thread is about release only. But logically after release (real quit), no more re…

