The problem: existing UT tests assert synchronously — `reporter.state(...)` then immediately `expect(sink, expected)`. With chaining, `_send` (and thus the recorder) runs a microtask later, so the sink is still empty at assertion time.

The docstring of `wired` says: "The recorder completes immediately — reports append SYNCHRONOUSLY at the state()/release() call, so test assertions need no pumping." The design intent of this suite is synchronous recording.

Options:

**Option A:** Keep `_spawn` synchronous when the previous link already completed. I.e., chain but run the first send synchronously:

```dart
void _spawn(List<String> argv) {
  if (!_active) return;
  if (_runProcess == null) return;
  if (_released) return;
  final pending = _pending;
  if (pending.isCompleted) {  // hmm — Future doesn't have isCompleted
```

Futures don't expose completion synchronously. Could track with a bool:

```dart
Future<void> _pending = Future<void>.value();
bool _deliveryIdle = true; // no report in flight

void _spawn(List<String> argv) {
  ...
  if (_deliveryIdle) {
    _deliveryIdle = false;
    _pending = _send(argv).whenComplete(() => _deliveryIdle = true);
    return;
  }
  _pending = _pending.then((_) => _send(argv));
}
```

Hmm wait — `_send(argv)` invoked synchronously → recorder called synchronously at state() → existing sync tests pass. whenComplete marks idle when done. Chained sends when busy.

But subtle: `_deliveryIdle = false; _pending = _send(argv).whenComplete(...)` — `_send` runs synchronously until its first await (`run(argv).timeout(timeout)` — the recorder is called synchronously inside `run(argv)` evaluation... actually `run(argv)` is invoked inside the `try` — synchronously — then `.timeout` wraps). So the recorder sees the argv during `state()`. ✓

When the first link completes (`whenComplete` fires), `_deliveryIdle = true` BUT any queued links: `_pending = _pending.then((_) => _send(argv))` — the queued `.then` fires after whenComplete? Order: `_pending` (first link with whenComplete) completes → its listeners fire in registration order: the whenComplete callback (sets idle true) registered FIRST (at spawn time), then subsequent `.then` registrations (later spawns). So whenComplete runs first, sets idle=true, then the queued `.then` starts the next `_send` — which will see... wait, `_deliveryIdle` isn't consulted by the chain; the chain runs `_send` directly. `_deliveryIdle` only gates the entry point in `_spawn` for NEW spawns. A new spawn during the queued-link window: `_deliveryIdle` is still false until the whenComplete of the CURRENT head runs... hmm, let me re-think.

Sequence: spawn A: idle=true→ call _send(A) sync; idle=false; _pending = sendA.whenComplete(idle→true).
spawn B (while A in flight): idle=false → `_pending = _pending.then((_) => _send(B))`. 
A completes → whenComplete fires → idle=true. Then the .then fires → _send(B) starts — but idle stays TRUE while B is in flight! (The whenComplete only guarded A.) Now spawn C arrives while B in flight → idle==true → calls _send(C) synchronously → CONCURRENT with B → ordering broken!

Fix: whenComplete must set idle=true only when the whole chain drained. Track queue depth:

```dart
int _inFlight = 0; // reports queued-but-not-finished

void _spawn(List<String> argv) {
  if (!_active) return;
  if (_runProcess == null) return;
  if (_released) return;
  _inFlight++;
  final send = () {
    _inFlight--;
    ... hmm
```

Getting complicated. Simpler robust approach:

```dart
void _spawn(List<String> argv) {
  if (!_active) return;
  if (_runProcess == null) return;
  if (_released) return;
  final pending = _pending;
  if (_pendingComplete) {
    // Nothing in flight: send synchronously (the recorder is invoked at
    // the call site, preserving the fire-and-forget timing) ...
    _pendingComplete = false;
    _pending = _send(argv).whenComplete(() => _pendingComplete = true);
  } else {
    _pending = pending.then((_) => _send(argv));
  }
```

Still has the B/C bug above: when B's link runs (after whenComplete set flag true), a C spawning between flag-set and B's send start... Can that interleave? The `.then` callback and `whenComplete` callback run in the same microtask drain: whenComplete is a listener on _pending; the chained `.then` is ALSO a listener on the same _pending future. Listeners fire in registration order — whenComplete registered when B was spawned (before the .then? No!). Order of registration on _pending(first-link future): 
- spawn A: registers whenComplete (via whenComplete call).
- spawn B: registers .then (chained).
So on completion: whenComplete (idle=true) fires, THEN .then → _send(B) starts. During both microtasks, no user code runs (microtask drain). A user spawn can only happen from event-loop code (timers, IO) — NOT between microtasks. So C's `_spawn` can only run after the microtask drain completes. By then, `_send(B)` has STARTED (it's in progress, recorder called synchronously in its first sync section... wait — `.then((_) => _send(B))` — the callback runs, `_send(B)` invoked, recorder called synchronously within it, returns in-flight future). But `_pendingComplete` flag: after whenComplete set it true, B's start does NOT set it false! So C would think the chain is idle and send concurrently with B. BUG.

Fix: reset the flag inside the chained send too:

```dart
Future<void> _chain(List<String> argv, {bool synchronous = false}) ...
```

Cleaner design: keep a single `_pending` future plus the invariant that `_pending` is completed ⇔ chain drained. Use a helper that runs the FIRST send synchronously only when the chain is drained. Implement drained-tracking correctly:

```dart
bool _chainIdle = true;
Future<void> _pending = Future<void>.value();

void _spawn(List<String> argv) {
  if (!_active) return;
  if (_runProcess == null) return;
  if (_released) return;
  if (_chainIdle) {
    // Fast path: nothing in flight — send synchronously (the recorder
    // observes the argv at the call site; the state() call stays
    // synchronous) and hold the chain-open flag until it settles.
    _chainIdle = false;
    _pending = _send(argv).then((_) {
      _chainIdle = true;
    });
    return;
  }
  _pending = _pending.then((_) {
    _chainIdle = false;
    return _send(argv);
  });
}
```

