{"content":"# Thread `PRRT_kwDOTXdlLc6rDoyO` — `release()` has no once-guard

**Fixed — guard adopted.** `HerdrReporter.release()` is now idempotent by construction:

```dart
Future<void> release() async {
  if (!_active || _released) return;
  _released = true;
  await _enqueue(releaseArgs(seq: _nextSeq()));
}
```

Beyond the once-guard, `_spawn` drops any report requested after the release — the pane registration is gone at that point, so late hooks can no longer spawn zombie reports.

Pinned by `test/cli/herdr_reporter_test.dart` group **"release once-guard"**: a double `release()` spawns exactly one `release-agent`, and reports after the release spawn nothing.
","path":"outputs/review_replies/thread_13_release_once_guard.md"}

{"content":"# Thread `PRRT_kwDOTXdlLc6rDoyz`→`PRRT_kwDOTXdlLc6rDozK` — `Process.start` without draining stdout/stderr

**Fixed — drains added, and the transport is now unit-pinned.** The spawn closure moved out of the `AgentCliConfig` call site into a standalone bin library, `bin/fah_herdr_spawn.dart` (imported by `bin/fah.dart`, wired as `herdrSpawn: spawnHerdrReport` — same testability pattern as `bin/fah_boot_restore.dart`):

```dart
final process = await Process.start(argv.first, argv.sublist(1));
unawaited(process.stdout.drain<void>());
unawaited(process.stderr.drain<void>());
await process.exitCode.timeout(killAfter, onTimeout: () {
  process.kill();
  return process.exitCode;
});
```

The kill-on-timeout contract is untouched (5s window under the reporter's 2s abandon); the drains are unawaited, so the function's own awaits stay the window and `exitCode` only.

Pinned by the new `test/bin_herdr_spawn_drain_test.dart` (`io`-tagged, real processes): a child emitting 192 KiB on stdout completes far under the kill window (a blocked pipe would have held it until the 5s kill — that is the assertion), and a hung child is killed inside a short configured window, pinning that the refactor preserved kill-on-timeout.
","path":"outputs/review_replies/thread_14_drained_stdio.md"}

